<!-- This file is precomputed: the Stan models take about a minute to
compile, so it is knitted locally from bayesian-calibration.Rmd.orig with
data-raw/knit_bayesian_vignette.R. Edit the .orig file, not the .Rmd. -->

```{r, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>",
                      fig.path = "bayes-figures/", dpi = 96)
```

The calibration of `vignette("calibration")` fits the candidate models by
maximum likelihood with 'lme4'. sheepdog can also fit the selected model in
a Bayesian framework with 'brms'. This gives three things the maximum
likelihood fit does not:

* the uncertainty of the calibrated probability of every detection, and a
  rule that uses it ("is P(TP) >= `p_min` with high posterior
  probability?") instead of a point estimate;
* priors that regularise the variance components, which avoids the
  singular fits that 'lme4' gives when a correlation or a variance is
  estimated at the boundary;
* the propagation of the calibration uncertainty to the detection
  histories and the community matrices.

The cost is computing time and the need to state, and check, a prior. The
workflow below follows the steps of a Bayesian workflow: prior predictive
check, fit, convergence diagnostics, posterior predictive check and
sensitivity to the prior (Gelman et al. 2026; McElreath 2020).

```{r}
library(sheepdog)
suppressPackageStartupMessages(library(dplyr))
ex <- system.file("extdata", "example", package = "sheepdog")
val_files <- list.files(file.path(ex, "validation"), full.names = TRUE)
val <- suppressMessages(read_validations(val_files))
```

## The model

We use the model retained by the log-loss rule in `vignette("calibration")`:
species slopes and intercepts without their correlation, and site x
species intercepts.

```{r}
f <- calibration_models(uncorrelated = TRUE)$m5_species_x_site_uncorrelated
f
```

## The prior

`fit_calibration(engine = "brms")` requires the prior to be given
explicitly: 'brms' would otherwise use flat priors on the slopes.
`calibration_prior()` builds a weakly informative example centred on the
hypothesis that the BirdNET score is already calibrated, P(TP) = score.
The data then show how far the classifier departs from it.

```{r}
prior <- calibration_prior(f, val)
prior
```

* The slope of `logit_conf` is centred on 1 (a calibrated score), with a
  standard deviation of 1, and truncated at 0: a higher score never lowers
  P(TP).
* 'brms' centres the predictors, so the intercept is the logit of P(TP) at
  the mean logit score of the reviewed clips
  (`r round(mean(val$logit_conf), 3)`); centring its prior on that value
  makes slope 1 and intercept equal to the mean correspond to P(TP) =
  score.
* The standard deviations of the species and site x species effects get
  `exponential(1)`, most of whose mass lies on moderate differences on the
  logit scale.

### Prior predictive check

Before fitting, sample the model from the prior alone and look at the
calibration curves it implies on the probability scale.
`plot_calibration_draws()` draws the population-level curve of 100 draws:

```{r prior-check, fig.width = 6, fig.height = 4.5}
prior_fit <- prior_predictive_calibration(
  f, val, prior, chains = 2, iter = 1000, refresh = 0, silent = 2,
  backend = "cmdstanr", seed = 2026)
plot_calibration_draws(prior_fit)
```

The prior allows curves well above and well below the diagonal (a
calibrated score), all increasing; it rules out nothing a reviewer would
consider plausible, and it does not force the curves to cross `p_min` at
any particular score.

## Fitting

```{r fit}
fit <- fit_calibration(val, f, engine = "brms", prior = prior,
                       chains = 4, cores = 4, iter = 2000,
                       control = list(adapt_delta = 0.95),
                       refresh = 0, silent = 2, backend = "cmdstanr",
                       seed = 2026)
```

`fit_calibration()` runs `calibration_diagnostics()` after the fit and
warns when the largest R-hat exceeds 1.01, the bulk or tail effective
sample sizes fall below 400, or there are divergent transitions:

```{r}
calibration_diagnostics(fit)
```

A higher `adapt_delta` (here 0.95) is the first remedy for divergent
transitions; persistent divergences point to a model that the data cannot
identify.

```{r}
summary(fit)$fixed
```

### Posterior predictive check

The observed proportion of true positives by species should fall within
the distribution of the proportions replicated from the posterior:

```{r ppc, fig.width = 7, fig.height = 5, message = FALSE}
brms::pp_check(fit, type = "stat_grouped", group = "species",
               stat = "mean", ndraws = 500)
```

### Sensitivity to the prior

`calibration_sensitivity()` runs the power-scaling analysis of
'priorsense' (Kallioinen et al. 2024): it measures how much each
population-level parameter and each standard deviation changes when the
prior or the likelihood is up- or down-weighted.

```{r}
sens <- calibration_sensitivity(fit)
sens
```

```{r, include = FALSE}
sens_df <- as.data.frame(sens)
flagged <- sens_df$variable[sens_df$diagnosis != "-"]
flag_b  <- grep("^b_", flagged, value = TRUE)
flag_sd <- grep("^sd_", flagged, value = TRUE)
```

A parameter sensitive to the prior but not to the likelihood
("strong prior / weak likelihood") is mostly determined by the prior; one
sensitive to both ("prior-likelihood conflict") is pulled in different
directions by the prior and the data. Here the flagged standard deviations
are `r if (length(flag_sd)) paste(flag_sd, collapse = ", ") else "none"`,
and the flagged population-level parameters are
`r if (length(flag_b)) paste(flag_b, collapse = ", ") else "none"`.
The standard deviations of the species effects, and the population slope
(the mean of the species slopes), are estimated from only
`r nlevels(val$species)` species, so their prior matters. Two things
follow. Report the prior together with the results. And check that the
conclusions (the calibrated probabilities near `p_min`, the detection
histories) do not change under a wider prior, for instance `normal(1, 2)`
on the slope: with 'priorsense' this is what the power-scaling
approximates without refitting. With more species, the likelihood takes
over and the flags disappear.

### Posterior calibration curves

```{r posterior-curves, fig.width = 6, fig.height = 4.5}
plot_calibration_draws(fit)
```

```{r, include = FALSE}
slope <- brms::fixef(fit)["logit_conf", ]
```

The population-level curves are steeper than the diagonal (posterior
mean slope `r round(slope["Estimate"], 2)`, 95 % interval
`r round(slope["Q2.5"], 2)` to `r round(slope["Q97.5"], 2)`): in the
example, low scores overestimate P(TP) and high scores underestimate it.
The curves by
species and site, with the observed precision by score bin, are drawn by
`plot_calibration()` as for the 'lme4' fit:

```{r curves, fig.width = 9, fig.height = 7}
plot_calibration(fit, val)
```

## Scoring the detections

`score_detections()` computes the posterior of P(TP) once on a grid of
scores (step 0.01) for each site x species, and assigns it to the
detections by their rounded score; the cost does not depend on the number
of detections. Each detection gets the posterior mean (`p_tp`), a lower
quantile (`p_tp_lower`, 5 % by default) and the posterior probability that
P(TP) >= `p_min` (`pr_reliable`):

```{r}
sites <- c("ARA", "BAI", "NHE", "XAR")
tables <- setNames(file.path(ex, "birdnet", sites,
                             "BirdNET_SelectionTable.txt"), sites)
scored <- suppressMessages(score_detections(tables, fit, p_min = 0.95))
scored |>
  select(site, species, confidence, p_tp, p_tp_lower, pr_reliable) |>
  arrange(desc(confidence)) |>
  head()
```

## Two rules for a reliable detection

`detection_history()` can count a detection as reliable when its posterior
mean reaches `p_min` (`rule = "mean"`, as with 'lme4') or when the
posterior probability that P(TP) >= `p_min` reaches `prob_min`
(`rule = "probability"`):

```{r}
h_mean <- detection_history(scored, p_min = 0.95)
h_prob <- detection_history(scored, p_min = 0.95, rule = "probability",
                            prob_min = 0.9)
tibble(rule = c("mean", "probability"),
       nights_k1 = c(sum(h_mean$detection_k1), sum(h_prob$detection_k1)),
       nights_k2 = c(sum(h_mean$detection_k2), sum(h_prob$detection_k2)))
```

The probability rule is more conservative where the calibration is
uncertain, typically in cells with few reviewed clips, because it asks
that the calibrated probability exceed `p_min` with high posterior
probability rather than on average.

```{r}
inner_join(count(h_mean, species, wt = detection_k1, name = "mean"),
           count(h_prob, species, wt = detection_k1, name = "probability"),
           by = "species")
```

The [choosing a route](https://provetelab.org/sheepdog/articles/choosing-a-route.html) article compares the two rules with the
empirical threshold and with one history per draw.

## Propagating the uncertainty to the community matrices

![The posterior of P(tp) gives one history per draw, and a mean and an interval per cell of the matrices.](figures/schematic_draws.png){width=100%}

Both rules still produce a single history. `history_draws()` rebuilds the
history for each of `n_draws` posterior draws: in a draw, a detection is
reliable when its probability in that draw is >= `p_min`. The cost is
again independent of the number of detections.

```{r}
hd <- history_draws(fit, scored, p_min = 0.95, n_draws = 200)
hd
```

`community_matrices_draws()` builds the community matrices of
`community_matrices()` for each draw and summarises each site x month x
species frequency by its posterior mean and a 90 % interval:

```{r}
core <- c("boa_ran", "ela_bic", "lep_pod", "phy_cuv")
md <- community_matrices_draws(hd, core, k_min = 2, min_nights_month = 5)
md$summary |>
  mutate(width = frequency_upper - frequency_lower) |>
  arrange(desc(width)) |>
  head()
```

```{r, include = FALSE}
w <- md$summary$frequency_upper - md$summary$frequency_lower
n_uncertain <- sum(w > 0)
```

`r n_uncertain` of the `r nrow(md$summary)` site x month x species cells
have a frequency that changes among draws; the others are fixed because
their detections are far above or below `p_min` in every draw.

```{r matrix-uncertainty, fig.width = 8, fig.height = 5}
library(ggplot2)
ggplot(md$summary, aes(month, frequency_mean, colour = site, group = site)) +
  geom_pointrange(aes(ymin = frequency_lower, ymax = frequency_upper),
                  position = position_dodge(width = 0.5), size = 0.3) +
  facet_wrap(~ species) +
  labs(x = NULL, y = "Frequency of nights with detection (k = 2)",
       subtitle = "Posterior mean and 90 % interval over 200 draws") +
  theme_minimal(base_size = 10) +
  theme(axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 45, hjust = 1))
```

`md$draws` keeps the frequency of every draw, so that a downstream
analysis (an ordination, a GLLVM; see `vignette("community-matrices")`)
can be repeated on each draw and its results summarised over draws.

## The calibrate() wrapper

`calibrate()` takes the same options. The comparison of the candidate
models still uses maximum likelihood, which is fast; the selected model is
then refitted with 'brms'. Passing `prior = calibration_prior` (the
function) builds the example prior for whichever model is selected:

```{r, eval = FALSE}
res <- calibrate(val_files, tables, output_dir = "calibration",
                 select = "one_se_logloss", uncorrelated = TRUE,
                 engine = "brms", prior = calibration_prior,
                 brms_args = list(chains = 4, cores = 4,
                                  control = list(adapt_delta = 0.95),
                                  backend = "cmdstanr"),
                 rule = "probability", prob_min = 0.9)
```

`calibrate()` then also writes `calibration_diagnostics.csv`.

## References

Gelman, A., Vehtari, A., McElreath, R., Simpson, D., Margossian, C. C.,
Yao, Y., Kennedy, L., Gabry, J., Bürkner, P.-C., Modrák, M., & Leos
Barajas, V. (2026). *Bayesian Workflow*. Chapman & Hall.
<https://avehtari.github.io/Bayesian-Workflow/>

Kallioinen, N., Paananen, T., Bürkner, P.-C., & Vehtari, A. (2024).
Detecting and diagnosing prior and likelihood sensitivity with
power-scaling. *Statistics and Computing*, 34, 57.
<https://doi.org/10.1007/s11222-023-10366-5>

McElreath, R. (2020). *Statistical Rethinking: A Bayesian Course with
Examples in R and Stan* (2nd ed.). Chapman and Hall/CRC.
<https://doi.org/10.1201/9780429029608>
